  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers will exit the area this evening.\n\nA mid level shortwave and associated surface trough will cross the \neastern Great Lakes through early evening. Moisture transport into \nthe area and convergence along the boundary will be enhanced on the \nnose of a 40 knot low level jet propagating across the eastern Great \nLakes this evening. Showers will rapidly end from west to east this \nevening as the trough moves into eastern NY. This will leave a \nmainly dry overnight, although a few spotty showers cannot be ruled \nout as warm advection increases. Patchy fog or low stratus may \ndevelop northeast of the Lakes overnight. Locations near the \nlakeshore may experience hazardous driving conditions early Sunday \nmorning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Summer-like heat Monday and Tuesday. \n\nSummer-like heat will build Monday behind a passing warm front \nSunday night. Speaking of which..it is quite possible we could even \nsee a shower or isolated thunderstorm with the passing of this warm \nfront. Have added very low end PoPs Sunday evening and night. \n\nBuilding heights aloft across the eastern 1/3 of the U.S in concert \nwith 850 hPa T's rising to +14 to +17 will support summer-like heat \non Monday across the Lower Lakes. In terms of temperatures, expect \n80s across much of western and north-central New York, with a few \nlowers 90s possible for some of our normally warmest spots. With \nbuilding afternoon instability it will possibly lend to a few \nshowers or an isolated thunderstorm, especially along lake breeze \nboundaries. While it will be warm, closer to the lakes it will be \ncooler given area water temperatures are still not all that warm and \nin the 40s to lower 50s. Take precautions if you plan to visit any \narea waterways next week.\n\nHeat continues Tuesday but we may be a few degrees cooler due in \nlarge part to some clouds moving in as the trough begins to near the \nregion. That said...as the trough nears we may begin to see some \nshowers and thunderstorms in the afternoon-evening. At this \npoint...it appears that the cold front will potentially hold off \npassing through the eastern Great Lakes until late Tuesday night or \neven early Wednesday. This will continue the threat for showers and \nthunderstorms. After the cold front clears the area we will see it \nturn cooler and drier, which will last through the end of the week.",
